    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Do you find that you frequently act on impulses, such as interrupting others during conversations or making decisions without thinking through the consequences?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S018",
      "patient_response": "Yeah, I definitely do that. Like, especially at work. If I think of an idea while someone else is working through a slide deck, I'll just pop over or interrupt their conversation to add my thought. Even when it probably could have waited. \n\nActually, that happened just yesterday with my coworker, Dave. He was going over this new campaign for a local car dealership, which, by the way, I think car commercials are just so repetitive. It's always the same booming voice over some guy driving down a winding mountain road. Like, do people even drive on roads like that? I live in the city, the only winding road I see is the line at the drive-thru. And speaking of the drive-thru, I went to that new taco place on 5th Street on my lunch break, and they completely messed up my order. I asked for no cilantro, and they practically buried my tacos in it. I have that gene, you know? The one that makes cilantro taste like soap. It's so weird how genetics work like that. My sister doesn't have it, she puts cilantro on everything. \n\nBut anyway... what were we talking about? Oh, right, interrupting. Yeah, I just blurt things out before I even realize I'm doing it. I just get so excited about an idea that I feel like I have to say it right then or I'll lose it forever.",
